# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in real values.
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored and must NEVER be committed. Real environment
# variables always win over this file, so CI secrets are never shadowed.
#
# In GitHub Actions these are repository secrets instead
# (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions), and each one must be listed
# in the capture workflow's `env:` block.

# Required. Who is running this capture, shown in the User-Agent of every
# request so a publisher can reach you instead of silently blocking you.
# Some APIs (SEC EDGAR, for one) reject requests without it.
#
# A GITHUB IDENTITY AND REPO URL is the best value: it identifies you, leads
# to an issue tracker, and contains no personal data. Every fork sets its own.
WSS_CONTACT={{OWNER}} +https://github.com/{{OWNER}}/{{NAME}}
# ...an email works too if you would rather be reachable directly, but it is
# transmitted to every publisher you capture from, so prefer a role address
# over a personal one:
# WSS_CONTACT=team@example.com

# One line per authenticated source, matching the `auth: {bearer_env: ...}`
# name in its registry entry. Name third-party credentials the way the
# publisher does (OPENROUTER_API_KEY); reserve WSS_* for engine config.
# Treat every one of these as a payment credential unless you know otherwise.
# SOMEPUBLISHER_API_KEY=
